1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:10,480
In 1869, the transcontinental railroad opens and America begins her love affair with trains.

2
00:00:10,481 --> 00:00:16,860
The next 30 years will be an era of invention, expansion, and empire building.

3
00:00:17,420 --> 00:00:20,560
A period of great romance and calamity.

4
00:00:21,260 --> 00:00:26,100
Now, the railroads that tame the West on Modern Marvels.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:35,000
The American Pronunciation Guide Presents «How to Pronounce Lincoln»

6
00:00:35,100 --> 00:00:42,080
At the start of the Civil War, Abraham Lincoln launched

7
00:00:42,180 --> 00:00:47,180
America's dream of connecting the country by rail.

8
00:00:47,280 --> 00:00:49,660
The first transcontinental railroad

9
00:00:49,760 --> 00:00:52,520
was the most ambitious engineering challenge

10
00:00:52,620 --> 00:00:55,900
of the 19th century.

11
00:00:56,000 --> 00:01:00,000
In 1863, the two railroad companies assigned to the task

12
00:01:00,001 --> 00:01:04,140
the Central Pacific Building East from Sacramento, California,

13
00:01:04,240 --> 00:01:08,340
and the Union Pacific Building West from Omaha, Nebraska,

14
00:01:08,440 --> 00:01:13,840
faced formidable obstacles building a 1,600 mile path

15
00:01:13,940 --> 00:01:17,820
through some of the most difficult terrain on Earth,

16
00:01:17,920 --> 00:01:23,860
including two mountain ranges and a vast desert wasteland.

17
00:01:23,960 --> 00:01:26,560
For six brutal years, an army of men

18
00:01:26,561 --> 00:01:29,460
accomplished some of the most spectacular engineering

19
00:01:29,560 --> 00:01:31,860
feats of the era.

20
00:01:31,960 --> 00:01:34,300
They chipped and blasted a half mile tunnel

21
00:01:34,400 --> 00:01:36,940
with homemade nitroglycerin, erected

22
00:01:37,040 --> 00:01:40,500
enormous wooden trestles, laid tracks

23
00:01:40,600 --> 00:01:42,780
through the great Western plains

24
00:01:42,880 --> 00:01:45,520
under threat of Indian attack.

25
00:01:45,620 --> 00:01:50,580
Hundreds of men were killed or injured on the job.

26
00:01:50,680 --> 00:01:53,260
When these rugged railroaders closed the tracks

27
00:01:53,360 --> 00:01:55,220
of the first transcontinental railroad

28
00:01:55,221 --> 00:01:59,920
at Promontory, Utah on May 10, 1869,

29
00:02:00,020 --> 00:02:04,160
they knew they had accomplished something wonderful,

30
00:02:04,260 --> 00:02:07,160
a national work that would last through the ages.

31
00:02:10,060 --> 00:02:13,280
But the challenge to tame the West was far from over.

32
00:02:13,380 --> 00:02:17,180
In fact, it had really only just begun.

33
00:02:17,280 --> 00:02:19,920
If the railroad was to survive, the owners

34
00:02:20,020 --> 00:02:23,620
would have to find a way of expanding it from just one thin

35
00:02:23,621 --> 00:02:27,760
line of steel to thousands and thousands of miles.

36
00:02:27,860 --> 00:02:31,620
Ahead lay dangers and challenges even they could not imagine.

37
00:02:38,400 --> 00:02:41,180
Five days after the joining of the two lines,

38
00:02:41,280 --> 00:02:43,940
regular train service began.

39
00:02:44,040 --> 00:02:46,980
What had taken four to six months by wagon now

40
00:02:47,080 --> 00:02:49,520
only took seven days.

41
00:02:49,620 --> 00:02:52,620
The early journey west was a magic carpet

42
00:02:52,621 --> 00:02:55,520
to a mysterious and wild new world.

43
00:02:55,620 --> 00:02:58,520
The passengers experienced awesome new landscapes

44
00:02:58,620 --> 00:03:05,060
and wildlife, antelope, prairie dogs, coyotes,

45
00:03:05,160 --> 00:03:08,460
and the last remaining buffalo.

46
00:03:08,560 --> 00:03:11,080
For many travelers, seeing the Rocky Mountains

47
00:03:11,180 --> 00:03:14,180
was a childhood dream come true.

48
00:03:14,280 --> 00:03:18,020
One man wrote, my boyish dreams were realized.

49
00:03:18,120 --> 00:03:21,520
For hours at the school desk, I have pondered over the map

50
00:03:21,521 --> 00:03:24,820
and wandered in imagination with Lewis and Clark,

51
00:03:24,920 --> 00:03:27,960
the hunters and trappers and early immigrants.

52
00:03:28,060 --> 00:03:32,500
And here lay the first great range in the pureness of white,

53
00:03:32,600 --> 00:03:38,000
distant to be sure, but there it lay, enshrined in beauty."

54
00:03:38,100 --> 00:03:42,000
Railroad Traveler, 1871.

55
00:03:42,100 --> 00:03:45,580
Passengers would also see a rougher, more hostile landscape

56
00:03:45,680 --> 00:03:47,840
from their window, a wasteland littered

57
00:03:47,940 --> 00:03:50,220
with painful reminders of pioneers

58
00:03:50,221 --> 00:03:53,420
who had come before them.

59
00:03:53,520 --> 00:03:56,420
The early passengers quickly learned that even their own

60
00:03:56,520 --> 00:04:00,360
journey west was no smooth ride to paradise.

61
00:04:00,460 --> 00:04:03,500
Railroad travel was dangerous in the early years.

62
00:04:03,600 --> 00:04:06,060
Train wrecks and derailments were common,

63
00:04:06,160 --> 00:04:11,880
killing and injuring countless passengers and railroad men.

64
00:04:11,980 --> 00:04:13,580
Boilers blew up.

65
00:04:13,680 --> 00:04:16,380
Bridges collapsed under the weight of trains.

66
00:04:16,480 --> 00:04:18,720
Brittle iron tracks cracked.

67
00:04:18,721 --> 00:04:22,220
Primitive brakes overheated and failed.

68
00:04:22,320 --> 00:04:24,320
The lack of a signal system sometimes

69
00:04:24,420 --> 00:04:27,160
caused two trains to be accidentally switched

70
00:04:27,260 --> 00:04:31,900
onto the same track and sent speeding into each other.

71
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:34,040
The wood passenger cars seemed almost

72
00:04:34,140 --> 00:04:37,180
to self-destruct on impact.

73
00:04:37,280 --> 00:04:39,900
Passengers could be crushed and thrown out.

74
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:42,140
Windows would shatter because there was no safety plate

75
00:04:42,240 --> 00:04:43,480
glass in that day.

76
00:04:43,580 --> 00:04:45,820
Stoves would overturn, spilling hot coals

77
00:04:45,920 --> 00:04:47,920
through the crushed and broken wood,

78
00:04:47,921 --> 00:04:50,420
and instantly trains were set on fire.

79
00:04:50,520 --> 00:04:54,820
Cars would fall into rivers off of bridges.

80
00:04:54,920 --> 00:04:57,260
Weather was a major problem.

81
00:04:57,360 --> 00:05:00,260
In snowstorms, the train could be stranded for days

82
00:05:00,360 --> 00:05:02,660
in the middle of nowhere.

83
00:05:02,760 --> 00:05:05,940
Often when we came to a stop, only the top of the stack

84
00:05:06,040 --> 00:05:08,700
or the front engine would be visible.

85
00:05:08,800 --> 00:05:12,620
All this time, the snow kept coming down day and night

86
00:05:12,720 --> 00:05:15,540
until the only signs of a railroad across the range

87
00:05:15,541 --> 00:05:18,880
were the tops of the telegraph poles.

88
00:05:18,980 --> 00:05:21,240
Cy Warman, 1870.

89
00:05:24,340 --> 00:05:27,060
Even in fair weather, the first transcontinental railroad

90
00:05:27,160 --> 00:05:30,020
running from Omaha, Nebraska to Sacramento, California

91
00:05:30,120 --> 00:05:30,920
had problems.

92
00:05:35,560 --> 00:05:37,060
In the race to build the line, there

93
00:05:37,160 --> 00:05:40,660
was naturally a long list of defects.

94
00:05:40,760 --> 00:05:43,660
Unsafe bridges, tunnels too narrow,

95
00:05:43,661 --> 00:05:47,900
roadbed not properly level, poor rail alignment,

96
00:05:48,000 --> 00:05:51,800
and carelessly laid cross ties.

97
00:05:51,900 --> 00:05:54,400
One government inspector declared it the worst railroad

98
00:05:54,500 --> 00:05:57,900
he had ever seen, but that didn't stop the railroaders.

99
00:06:03,820 --> 00:06:07,720
Railroad men risked life and limb on their job.

100
00:06:07,820 --> 00:06:12,420
Many would end up dead or permanently crippled.

101
00:06:12,421 --> 00:06:15,920
More than six times as many railroad men as passengers

102
00:06:16,020 --> 00:06:18,120
were killed or injured in accidents

103
00:06:18,220 --> 00:06:23,360
during the early days of the Transcontinental Railroad.

104
00:06:23,460 --> 00:06:28,800
Railroading ranked as one of America's most dangerous jobs.

105
00:06:28,900 --> 00:06:32,000
Railroads didn't so much care about the safety aspects.

106
00:06:32,100 --> 00:06:33,480
They were concerned about the bottom line

107
00:06:33,580 --> 00:06:34,880
and the dollar and the profit.

108
00:06:34,980 --> 00:06:37,540
So the less they had to change, the better.

109
00:06:37,640 --> 00:06:42,420
The fireman worked hard for his $2.40 a day.

110
00:06:42,421 --> 00:06:46,420
Early engines burned anywhere from 40 to 200 pounds

111
00:06:46,421 --> 00:06:49,220
of coal per mile.

112
00:06:49,221 --> 00:06:52,740
Sometimes a fireman might move more than two tons of coal

113
00:06:52,741 --> 00:06:55,900
in less than half an hour.

114
00:06:55,901 --> 00:06:58,800
One danger that was unique to Western railroading

115
00:06:58,801 --> 00:07:00,340
was buffalo.

116
00:07:00,341 --> 00:07:02,080
The buffalo at that time were a problem.

117
00:07:02,081 --> 00:07:05,300
They would get on the tracks, preventing the train

118
00:07:05,301 --> 00:07:06,820
from progressing forward.

119
00:07:06,821 --> 00:07:09,540
You can imagine 1,000 buffalo on the tracks.

120
00:07:09,541 --> 00:07:12,420
On one occasion, the story goes that the buffalo

121
00:07:12,421 --> 00:07:13,920
stopped this train.

122
00:07:14,020 --> 00:07:17,120
The engineer was incensed with those animals on the track.

123
00:07:17,220 --> 00:07:19,260
And so he took a shot at them.

124
00:07:19,360 --> 00:07:24,160
They stampeded and derailed the locomotive.

125
00:07:24,260 --> 00:07:26,500
The railroad companies quickly realized

126
00:07:26,600 --> 00:07:29,640
that if the railroad was going to expand in the West,

127
00:07:29,740 --> 00:07:32,300
they needed to be faster and safer.

128
00:07:32,400 --> 00:07:34,780
Thousands of people rose to the challenge,

129
00:07:34,880 --> 00:07:38,920
but only a few would know success.

130
00:07:39,020 --> 00:07:41,940
Daniel Webster once said about building railroads

131
00:07:41,941 --> 00:07:43,200
in the West.

132
00:07:43,300 --> 00:07:47,240
What do we want with this region of savages and wild beasts,

133
00:07:47,340 --> 00:07:52,380
of deserts of shifting sands, and whirlwinds of dust, cactus,

134
00:07:52,480 --> 00:07:53,480
and prairie dogs?

135
00:07:57,980 --> 00:08:01,500
As railroaders braved incredible dangers on the job,

136
00:08:01,600 --> 00:08:04,500
thousands of inventors from all walks of life

137
00:08:04,600 --> 00:08:08,200
were turning their attention to fixing the technology.

138
00:08:08,300 --> 00:08:09,700
In the mid-19th century, you could

139
00:08:09,701 --> 00:08:11,360
you could think of the railroad industry

140
00:08:11,361 --> 00:08:14,540
as being a combination of the automobile industry,

141
00:08:14,541 --> 00:08:17,980
the computer industry, and the television industry of our day.

142
00:08:17,981 --> 00:08:19,920
So that that's where the money was.

143
00:08:19,921 --> 00:08:21,420
That's where the inventors went.

144
00:08:21,421 --> 00:08:23,440
That's where men went to make their fortunes

145
00:08:23,441 --> 00:08:27,480
to create this industrial empire.

146
00:08:27,481 --> 00:08:30,120
The area of most intense interest

147
00:08:30,121 --> 00:08:33,380
was the inventions that made trains faster, more reliable,

148
00:08:33,381 --> 00:08:34,081
and safer.

149
00:08:34,082 --> 00:08:40,260
The coupler, the device that joined railroad cars together,

150
00:08:40,360 --> 00:08:44,160
attracted more inventors than any other railroad device.

151
00:08:44,260 --> 00:08:46,660
Whoever came up with the idea of how

152
00:08:46,760 --> 00:08:52,240
to make a safer and better coupler stood to make a fortune.

153
00:08:52,340 --> 00:08:54,180
One inventor tackling the problem

154
00:08:54,280 --> 00:08:58,120
was an ex-Confederate Civil War major who worked in a dry goods

155
00:08:58,220 --> 00:09:00,580
store in Alexandria, Virginia.

156
00:09:00,581 --> 00:09:03,980
In his free time, Eli Janney took pen to paper,

157
00:09:04,080 --> 00:09:05,680
searching for a solution.

158
00:09:08,680 --> 00:09:11,220
At that time, the standard method of coupling cars

159
00:09:11,320 --> 00:09:14,320
was the link and pin system.

160
00:09:14,420 --> 00:09:17,800
It called for the brakeman to stand between the two cars.

161
00:09:17,900 --> 00:09:20,060
And while one of the cars was in motion,

162
00:09:20,160 --> 00:09:24,100
he had to hold and guide the pin into the iron hoop of the links

163
00:09:24,200 --> 00:09:26,600
as they came together.

164
00:09:26,700 --> 00:09:28,600
There was a split second of timing

165
00:09:28,601 --> 00:09:34,920
of timing wherein you had to slip this into the pocket hold it up and drop the pin and get your

166
00:09:34,921 --> 00:09:42,040
fingers extricated from that space before the car couplings literally touched if that happened and

167
00:09:42,041 --> 00:09:49,000
your fingers weren't clear no fingers sometimes no hands if a slip and fall occurred a man could

168
00:09:49,001 --> 00:09:58,040
be trapped in between the cars and gored crushed maimed killed the technology was so dangerous

169
00:09:58,041 --> 00:10:00,980
that virtually every brakeman was maimed.

170
00:10:00,981 --> 00:10:04,080
Back then, a railroad foreman could size up an applicant

171
00:10:04,081 --> 00:10:07,080
by looking at his hands.

172
00:10:07,081 --> 00:10:09,020
When the yardmaster or the trainmaster

173
00:10:09,021 --> 00:10:11,860
would ask the brakeman to see his hands,

174
00:10:11,861 --> 00:10:14,800
if he had all his digits, he was a greenhorn.

175
00:10:14,801 --> 00:10:17,700
If he had one missing, he had a little experience.

176
00:10:17,701 --> 00:10:20,260
If he had a couple missing, he was pretty good.

177
00:10:20,261 --> 00:10:21,760
But if he had all his fingers missing,

178
00:10:21,761 --> 00:10:24,140
they would make a signalman out of him or some job

179
00:10:24,141 --> 00:10:25,800
that he didn't have to use his hands.

180
00:10:28,040 --> 00:10:31,680
One day, Eli Janney sketched out a simple design for a coupler.

181
00:10:34,380 --> 00:10:37,280
His idea was for a kind of knuckle coupler that brought

182
00:10:37,380 --> 00:10:42,280
the cars together like two hands grasping each other.

183
00:10:42,380 --> 00:10:45,920
A lever running outside the car operated it so Brakeman

184
00:10:46,020 --> 00:10:50,400
would no longer have to stand between the cars.

185
00:10:50,500 --> 00:10:55,240
Janney's first patent issued in April 1868 came to nothing,

186
00:10:55,340 --> 00:10:58,040
but he didn't give up.

187
00:10:58,041 --> 00:11:00,940
Five years later, he patented an improved design

188
00:11:01,040 --> 00:11:04,180
that was sheer genius.

189
00:11:04,280 --> 00:11:06,280
The crucial advantage of Janie's new coupler

190
00:11:06,380 --> 00:11:10,120
was its ability to allow cars to easily couple,

191
00:11:10,220 --> 00:11:14,060
even when they were not at the exact same levels.

192
00:11:14,160 --> 00:11:16,900
But he didn't stop there.

193
00:11:17,000 --> 00:11:18,960
The practice at that time was for inventors

194
00:11:19,060 --> 00:11:23,000
to sell their new technology directly to railroad companies.

195
00:11:23,100 --> 00:11:25,160
They had to take their invention and actually

196
00:11:25,161 --> 00:11:28,560
actually go from railroad office to railroad office,

197
00:11:28,660 --> 00:11:34,500
begging, pleading, advertising, and promoting.

198
00:11:34,600 --> 00:11:36,400
The railroads were very conservative.

199
00:11:36,500 --> 00:11:41,200
And they were constantly looking for better inventions,

200
00:11:41,300 --> 00:11:42,320
better equipment.

201
00:11:42,420 --> 00:11:44,520
But they're also very careful about many

202
00:11:44,620 --> 00:11:46,220
of the harebrained schemes.

203
00:11:46,320 --> 00:11:47,520
What Jannie was able to do, though,

204
00:11:47,620 --> 00:11:51,860
is demonstrate a workable, relatively inexpensive piece

205
00:11:51,960 --> 00:11:54,360
of metal that dramatically improved

206
00:11:54,361 --> 00:11:57,700
the safety of passenger trains at first.

207
00:11:57,800 --> 00:11:59,960
At the same time Janie was perfecting

208
00:12:00,060 --> 00:12:02,760
his automatic coupler, another inventor

209
00:12:02,860 --> 00:12:06,580
was determined to overhaul the railroad's braking system.

210
00:12:06,680 --> 00:12:08,980
George Westinghouse grew up literally

211
00:12:09,080 --> 00:12:12,620
in his father's machine shop in Schenectady, New York,

212
00:12:12,720 --> 00:12:17,220
working with men who knew how to make things.

213
00:12:17,320 --> 00:12:19,460
Back then, the braking on trains was not done

214
00:12:19,560 --> 00:12:22,360
by the engineer in the cab, but by hand

215
00:12:22,361 --> 00:12:25,420
in a dangerous maneuver where one slip could cost the brake

216
00:12:25,520 --> 00:12:27,400
man his life.

217
00:12:27,500 --> 00:12:31,300
We had to ride out on top when the train was moving.

218
00:12:31,400 --> 00:12:34,400
That took nerve, coordination, timing,

219
00:12:34,500 --> 00:12:37,300
and a perfect sense of balance to go over the top of a freight

220
00:12:37,400 --> 00:12:38,500
train.

221
00:12:38,600 --> 00:12:44,320
Winter or summer, rain, snow, sleet, ice all over the roofs,

222
00:12:44,420 --> 00:12:46,920
and on brake wheels and handholds.

223
00:12:47,020 --> 00:12:50,420
Dick Nelson, 1871.

224
00:12:50,421 --> 00:12:54,760
Here was one more way that brakemen could lose their lives.

225
00:12:54,860 --> 00:12:57,400
Not only were the handbrakes hard to reach,

226
00:12:57,500 --> 00:13:00,560
they were difficult to operate.

227
00:13:00,660 --> 00:13:04,900
Uneven braking could cause a train to separate.

228
00:13:05,000 --> 00:13:06,900
If brakes were applied too tightly,

229
00:13:07,000 --> 00:13:08,940
friction heated the wheels, causing them

230
00:13:09,040 --> 00:13:13,940
to slide dangerously along the track.

231
00:13:14,040 --> 00:13:16,240
Brakemen would be forced to make quick adjustments

232
00:13:16,340 --> 00:13:18,880
on several cars to keep the train from speeding

233
00:13:18,881 --> 00:13:21,380
out of control.

234
00:13:21,480 --> 00:13:24,980
George Westinghouse explored ways of improving the braking

235
00:13:25,080 --> 00:13:28,420
system by using compressed air.

236
00:13:28,520 --> 00:13:32,120
The idea of using air to somehow stop trains and control

237
00:13:32,220 --> 00:13:35,560
their speed had been around for decades.

238
00:13:35,660 --> 00:13:36,800
There had been steam brakes.

239
00:13:36,900 --> 00:13:39,140
There had been very crude air brakes before that.

240
00:13:39,240 --> 00:13:41,400
But they were neither reliable, nor did they really work

241
00:13:41,500 --> 00:13:43,380
together as a system.

242
00:13:43,480 --> 00:13:47,340
Westinghouse became aware of the use of compressed air

243
00:13:47,341 --> 00:13:51,820
to run drills in tunnels in Europe, long railroad tunnels,

244
00:13:51,920 --> 00:13:55,480
and thought that if they could run air through a rubber hose

245
00:13:55,580 --> 00:13:58,320
5,000 feet in Europe to run a drill, well, by God,

246
00:13:58,420 --> 00:14:01,120
he could run air through a hose underneath a train

247
00:14:01,220 --> 00:14:05,760
and use it to stop the rolling motion.

248
00:14:05,860 --> 00:14:08,620
Westinghouse's breakthrough for an automatic break

249
00:14:08,720 --> 00:14:11,500
was that each car would carry its own reservoir

250
00:14:11,600 --> 00:14:14,560
of compressed air.

251
00:14:14,660 --> 00:14:16,700
The engineer with a valve in the cab

252
00:14:16,701 --> 00:14:19,340
could regulate the air pressure in a train line,

253
00:14:19,440 --> 00:14:22,580
which was a continuous pipe running the length of the train,

254
00:14:22,680 --> 00:14:25,340
connecting the air brake control valves on each car.

255
00:14:28,320 --> 00:14:30,880
By regulating the pressure in that train line,

256
00:14:30,980 --> 00:14:33,580
the engineer could apply and release all the brakes

257
00:14:33,680 --> 00:14:37,460
on that train simultaneously.

258
00:14:37,560 --> 00:14:40,160
Westinghouse built his first automatic air brake

259
00:14:40,260 --> 00:14:45,020
factory in Pittsburgh, Pennsylvania in 1875.

260
00:14:45,021 --> 00:14:48,620
Both Janie's automatic coupler and Westinghouse's air brake

261
00:14:48,720 --> 00:14:51,660
promised a far safer future for railroading.

262
00:14:51,760 --> 00:14:54,520
But in a cold financial calculation,

263
00:14:54,620 --> 00:14:56,840
both inventions were slow to be adopted

264
00:14:56,940 --> 00:14:58,500
by the railroad industry.

265
00:14:58,600 --> 00:15:01,540
It came down to dollars and cents.

266
00:15:01,640 --> 00:15:04,540
The railroad could hire a brakeman for $1.50 a day.

267
00:15:04,640 --> 00:15:07,180
If that brakeman got hurt or killed,

268
00:15:07,280 --> 00:15:08,740
just about their only responsibility

269
00:15:08,840 --> 00:15:11,220
was to move the body off the tracks and send it home

270
00:15:11,320 --> 00:15:12,520
in a basket.

271
00:15:12,620 --> 00:15:14,720
It was cheaper on the whole for the railroads just

272
00:15:14,721 --> 00:15:18,380
just to continue maiming and killing its employees

273
00:15:18,381 --> 00:15:22,220
than it was to actually invest millions of dollars

274
00:15:22,221 --> 00:15:25,160
in these new technologies.

275
00:15:25,161 --> 00:15:27,160
Perhaps the strongest motivation

276
00:15:27,161 --> 00:15:29,640
for the railroad companies to install air brakes

277
00:15:29,641 --> 00:15:33,200
and automatic couplers was the public outcry.

278
00:15:33,201 --> 00:15:36,080
People were growing more outraged by the carnage

279
00:15:36,081 --> 00:15:37,740
on the rails.

280
00:15:37,741 --> 00:15:41,480
At last, in 1893, Congress passed the Railroad Safety

281
00:15:41,481 --> 00:15:47,620
Appliance Act that required the railroads to apply the air brakes, automatic couplers,

282
00:15:47,621 --> 00:15:52,420
and other safety features.

283
00:15:52,421 --> 00:15:57,320
It was a watershed piece of legislation and the beginning of the process of regulating

284
00:15:57,321 --> 00:15:59,540
industrial safety.

285
00:15:59,541 --> 00:16:04,420
In an ironic twist in history, this safety technology that the railroads feared would

286
00:16:04,421 --> 00:16:10,160
cost too much actually improved productivity and saved money.

287
00:16:10,161 --> 00:16:15,440
automatic coupler made it possible to hitch up cars faster and without the need for human

288
00:16:15,441 --> 00:16:20,800
intervention in each coupling, while the air brakes permitted the railroads to operate longer

289
00:16:20,801 --> 00:16:23,855
and heavier trains far more reliably.

290
00:16:23,856 --> 00:16:28,480
While Janie and Westinghouse were worrying about safety,

291
00:16:28,481 --> 00:16:34,316
there was another man who dreamed of something far grander, luxury.

292
00:16:34,317 --> 00:16:36,320
Railroads created a new

293
00:16:36,321 --> 00:16:37,560
caste system.

294
00:16:37,660 --> 00:16:40,460
The wrong side of the tracks referred to the direction

295
00:16:40,560 --> 00:16:43,760
the sooty black engine smoke was blown by the wind.

296
00:16:49,160 --> 00:16:52,440
In the 1870s, affluent East Coast types

297
00:16:52,540 --> 00:16:55,800
rented private railroad cars for hunting expeditions

298
00:16:55,900 --> 00:16:57,780
in the Wild West.

299
00:16:57,880 --> 00:17:01,120
This luxury would cost $300 per week.

300
00:17:01,220 --> 00:17:05,320
Jerome Marble, a Massachusetts manufacturer and sportsman,

301
00:17:05,321 --> 00:17:08,360
hired one of the earliest hunting cars for his family

302
00:17:08,460 --> 00:17:11,460
to camp on the Dakota Plains.

303
00:17:11,560 --> 00:17:14,660
The car came fully loaded with gun racks, dog kennels,

304
00:17:14,760 --> 00:17:18,960
and large ice boxes to preserve trophies of the hunt.

305
00:17:19,060 --> 00:17:21,160
The mobile hunting lodge was just one

306
00:17:21,260 --> 00:17:23,780
of the more extravagant innovations

307
00:17:23,880 --> 00:17:27,180
of a man who was determined to make his fortune on railroad

308
00:17:27,280 --> 00:17:32,680
passenger travel, George Mortimer Pullman.

309
00:17:32,681 --> 00:17:36,020
Like so many other great late 19th century men,

310
00:17:36,120 --> 00:17:38,820
George Pullman was from the Northeast,

311
00:17:38,920 --> 00:17:45,120
born in Brockton, New York, on March 3, 1831.

312
00:17:45,220 --> 00:17:48,860
As a young man, he headed to Chicago to seek his fortune.

313
00:17:48,960 --> 00:17:52,340
Even before ground was broken for the first transcontinental

314
00:17:52,440 --> 00:17:56,200
railroad, Pullman realized that long distance travelers

315
00:17:56,300 --> 00:17:59,240
would need comfort to make the journey tolerable.

316
00:17:59,241 --> 00:18:02,040
tolerable.

317
00:18:02,140 --> 00:18:04,480
On one of his trips west from New York State,

318
00:18:04,580 --> 00:18:07,820
on his way to Chicago, George Pullman rented a sleeping car

319
00:18:07,920 --> 00:18:10,780
berth for the dollar it cost and climbed into it just

320
00:18:10,880 --> 00:18:12,160
to see how bad it was.

321
00:18:12,260 --> 00:18:14,560
And it was dark and cramped and small.

322
00:18:14,660 --> 00:18:16,560
And he thought it was atrocious.

323
00:18:16,660 --> 00:18:18,620
And legend has it that at that point,

324
00:18:18,720 --> 00:18:22,860
he decided that was a way he could make his fortune.

325
00:18:22,960 --> 00:18:27,160
His lifetime ambition was born to replicate the elegance

326
00:18:27,161 --> 00:18:31,440
and comfort of first class hotels on wheels.

327
00:18:31,540 --> 00:18:34,140
Other innovators experimented with sleepers,

328
00:18:34,240 --> 00:18:36,700
but Pullman was the one who came up with the idea

329
00:18:36,800 --> 00:18:40,500
of hinged upper berths that could be folded against the ceiling

330
00:18:40,600 --> 00:18:42,880
during the day, and the reclining seat

331
00:18:42,980 --> 00:18:44,140
could double as a bed.

332
00:18:47,740 --> 00:18:52,420
In 1864, he invested his entire savings of $20,000

333
00:18:52,520 --> 00:18:57,160
to build the magnificent sleeper car he called the Pioneer.

334
00:18:57,161 --> 00:19:00,460
No expense was spared in the craftsmanship.

335
00:19:00,560 --> 00:19:03,960
Each Pullman sleeping car mirrored the ornate Victorian

336
00:19:04,060 --> 00:19:06,800
style that was sweeping America.

337
00:19:06,900 --> 00:19:09,800
By the time transcontinental travel became popular,

338
00:19:09,900 --> 00:19:13,380
George Pullman had the market for overnight luxury

339
00:19:13,480 --> 00:19:15,820
railroading sewn up.

340
00:19:15,920 --> 00:19:19,520
In the 1870s, he was leasing to the Western lines

341
00:19:19,620 --> 00:19:24,820
his own fleet of cars, fully equipped and staffed.

342
00:19:24,821 --> 00:19:27,520
In 1870, the price of a first-class ticket

343
00:19:27,620 --> 00:19:30,600
between Omaha and Sacramento was $100,

344
00:19:30,700 --> 00:19:34,560
equivalent to over $3,000 today.

345
00:19:34,660 --> 00:19:37,040
Providing comfort was not the only problem

346
00:19:37,140 --> 00:19:39,440
that Pullman decided to tackle.

347
00:19:39,540 --> 00:19:41,140
People needed to eat.

348
00:19:41,240 --> 00:19:44,180
In the early years of transcontinental rail travel,

349
00:19:44,280 --> 00:19:47,000
passengers ate their meals during short stops

350
00:19:47,100 --> 00:19:50,120
at stations along the way.

351
00:19:50,220 --> 00:19:54,820
We found the quality of the food on the whole bad.

352
00:19:54,821 --> 00:19:57,160
All three meals were almost identical.

353
00:19:57,260 --> 00:20:00,960
Tea, buffalo steaks, antelope chops, sweet potatoes,

354
00:20:01,060 --> 00:20:06,160
and boiled Indian corn with hoe cakes and syrup ad nauseum.

355
00:20:06,260 --> 00:20:10,660
William Robertson, 1873.

356
00:20:10,760 --> 00:20:12,580
One traveler, having dined happily

357
00:20:12,680 --> 00:20:15,580
on a delicious chicken stew for breakfast,

358
00:20:15,680 --> 00:20:20,380
was later upset to learn that he had eaten prairie dog.

359
00:20:20,480 --> 00:20:22,980
Pullman guessed that this was no way to attract

360
00:20:22,981 --> 00:20:26,080
the rich clientele he was aiming for.

361
00:20:26,180 --> 00:20:30,620
In 1868, he introduced the most elegant dining car of its day,

362
00:20:30,720 --> 00:20:34,520
named the Delmonico, after a famous restaurant owner

363
00:20:34,620 --> 00:20:37,420
in New York City.

364
00:20:37,520 --> 00:20:40,840
The Pullman staff turned out 12 course meals.

365
00:20:40,940 --> 00:20:44,140
Women were proud to have gained 5 to 10 pounds

366
00:20:44,240 --> 00:20:48,780
on a one month round trip journey aboard a Hotel Express.

367
00:20:48,880 --> 00:20:52,740
The foods were the best of ducks and fowl.

368
00:20:52,741 --> 00:21:01,220
roast beef, roast ham, boiled ham, fresh potatoes, fresh baked breads, desserts made on board,

369
00:21:01,660 --> 00:21:09,220
even fresh Rocky Mountain trout when crossing over the Continental Divide caught and delivered directly to the train.

370
00:21:09,800 --> 00:21:14,700
Most of the cars even carried ice and could serve ice cream and exotic frozen desserts,

371
00:21:15,220 --> 00:21:19,600
which for people traveling across the desert in the late 19th century was nothing short of miraculous.

372
00:21:19,601 --> 00:21:26,940
With first-class business booming, Pullman built cars for smoking, reading, listening

373
00:21:26,941 --> 00:21:30,020
to music, and lounging.

374
00:21:30,021 --> 00:21:36,360
The decor included etched and stained plate glass, plush seat cushions, marble and walnut

375
00:21:36,361 --> 00:21:44,300
washstands, damask curtains, and massive mirrors and frames of gilded walnut.

376
00:21:44,301 --> 00:21:47,240
The Pullman cars were a symbol of America's manufacturing

377
00:21:47,340 --> 00:21:52,080
supremacy, offering unrivaled elegance.

378
00:21:52,180 --> 00:21:55,380
But not all travelers were as enchanted with their journeys.

379
00:22:02,480 --> 00:22:04,280
The people with the roughest rides

380
00:22:04,380 --> 00:22:07,220
were the millions of third class passengers who

381
00:22:07,320 --> 00:22:13,860
rode in cramped and stifling coaches known as immigrant cars.

382
00:22:13,861 --> 00:22:18,340
These trains earned their name for the role they played in America.

383
00:22:18,440 --> 00:22:21,200
Throughout the 19th and early 20th century,

384
00:22:21,300 --> 00:22:24,040
the railroad took the millions of immigrants pouring

385
00:22:24,140 --> 00:22:27,240
into the East Coast ports and distributed them

386
00:22:27,340 --> 00:22:29,680
out across the continent.

387
00:22:29,780 --> 00:22:32,480
There was no other way we could have settled this land

388
00:22:32,580 --> 00:22:35,620
without the immigrants or without the railroads.

389
00:22:35,720 --> 00:22:38,720
Their problem, though, was that they needed to go cheaply.

390
00:22:38,820 --> 00:22:39,920
They didn't care about time.

391
00:22:40,020 --> 00:22:42,120
They weren't concerned with comfort.

392
00:22:42,121 --> 00:22:44,280
But they needed to get where they were going for a penny

393
00:22:44,380 --> 00:22:47,360
a mile or even less.

394
00:22:47,460 --> 00:22:50,860
As a young man, the Scottish writer Robert Louis Stevenson

395
00:22:50,960 --> 00:22:54,960
rode west in an immigrant train.

396
00:22:55,060 --> 00:22:58,160
In contrast to the elegance of Pullman cars,

397
00:22:58,260 --> 00:23:02,480
Stevenson's compartment was overcrowded, unsanitary,

398
00:23:02,580 --> 00:23:06,880
and positively bone rattling, more fit for transporting

399
00:23:06,980 --> 00:23:08,720
cattle than people.

400
00:23:08,820 --> 00:23:11,880
Travelers sat on unpadded wooden benches for days

401
00:23:11,881 --> 00:23:13,340
on end.

402
00:23:13,440 --> 00:23:17,020
He wrote how the car was a long, narrow, wooden box,

403
00:23:17,120 --> 00:23:21,520
like a flat-roofed Noah's Ark, with a stove at one end

404
00:23:21,620 --> 00:23:23,320
and a toilet on the other.

405
00:23:23,420 --> 00:23:26,860
The benches are too short for anything but a young child.

406
00:23:26,960 --> 00:23:29,900
With a scarce elbow room for two to sit,

407
00:23:30,000 --> 00:23:32,840
there would not be space enough for one to lie.

408
00:23:32,940 --> 00:23:35,740
Robert Louis Stevenson.

409
00:23:35,840 --> 00:23:38,300
The aspiring writer arrived in California

410
00:23:38,400 --> 00:23:41,180
after 10 bone-jarring days, sick as a dog,

411
00:23:41,181 --> 00:23:44,820
but immensely relieved.

412
00:23:44,920 --> 00:23:48,060
What enticed people to take the train west?

413
00:23:48,160 --> 00:23:52,060
The answer was a combination of opportunities and freedom

414
00:23:52,160 --> 00:23:56,800
and the railroad company's shrewd promotional campaign.

415
00:23:56,900 --> 00:24:00,660
For the railroad to fully exploit its potential in the West,

416
00:24:00,760 --> 00:24:04,200
the railroad bosses needed to attract not just thousands,

417
00:24:04,300 --> 00:24:05,980
but millions of settlers.

418
00:24:05,981 --> 00:24:12,980
Pullman porters were originally ex-slaves hired by the Pullman Palace Car Company in 1867.

419
00:24:12,981 --> 00:24:20,980
Fifty years later, they formed the first African American Union, the Brotherhood of Sleeping Car Porters.

420
00:24:25,980 --> 00:24:35,980
In the two decades following the opening of the first Pacific Line, dozens of other railroad companies stepped up to the plate to fully export the

421
00:24:35,981 --> 00:24:39,380
the West.

422
00:24:39,480 --> 00:24:42,880
Everyone wanted to own a railroad that ended with the name

423
00:24:42,980 --> 00:24:43,980
Pacific.

424
00:24:44,080 --> 00:24:47,720
At stake were vast fortunes to be made from freight and

425
00:24:47,820 --> 00:24:49,220
passenger traffic.

426
00:24:49,320 --> 00:24:53,040
Four major transcontinental railroads, the Atchison,

427
00:24:53,140 --> 00:24:59,780
Topeka, and Santa Fe, the Southern Pacific, the Northern

428
00:24:59,880 --> 00:25:02,980
Pacific, and the Great Northern Railway would all

429
00:25:02,981 --> 00:25:04,580
chart new paths westward.

430
00:25:07,380 --> 00:25:09,420
Thousands of miles of shorter lines

431
00:25:09,520 --> 00:25:12,580
would be built to exploit the West's potential,

432
00:25:12,680 --> 00:25:15,020
the vast tracts of fertile soil,

433
00:25:15,120 --> 00:25:17,720
millions of acres of virgin forests,

434
00:25:17,820 --> 00:25:22,940
mineral deposits such as gold, silver, and copper.

435
00:25:23,040 --> 00:25:25,040
To encourage building, the government

436
00:25:25,140 --> 00:25:27,240
gave railroad companies land grants

437
00:25:27,340 --> 00:25:31,540
totaling over 155 million acres with the right

438
00:25:31,541 --> 00:25:35,540
to sell it at a profit.

439
00:25:35,640 --> 00:25:38,880
In the mid and late 19th century, railroad executives

440
00:25:38,980 --> 00:25:41,780
engaged in an all-out battle with their competitors

441
00:25:41,880 --> 00:25:45,680
to expand their territory.

442
00:25:45,780 --> 00:25:47,620
Fighting on the front line of the battles

443
00:25:47,720 --> 00:25:50,600
were the survey teams, each one out there

444
00:25:50,700 --> 00:25:54,460
for their own railroad, trying to stake out their own route,

445
00:25:54,560 --> 00:25:58,560
the best route, sometimes racing to stake a claim

446
00:25:58,660 --> 00:26:00,500
on the same mountain pass.

447
00:26:00,501 --> 00:26:03,880
in Colorado, in New Mexico, throughout the Rockies,

448
00:26:04,400 --> 00:26:06,400
these survey teams would often tangle with each other.

449
00:26:06,640 --> 00:26:09,620
There was gunfire, there was intrigue in the night,

450
00:26:09,800 --> 00:26:12,520
men staking out routes by torchlight and bonfires,

451
00:26:12,960 --> 00:26:14,600
trying to get there before the other guy.

452
00:26:15,420 --> 00:26:18,420
And in that still relatively lawless area,

453
00:26:18,760 --> 00:26:20,040
just about anything went.

454
00:26:20,440 --> 00:26:22,280
The person who got there first with the most

455
00:26:22,281 --> 00:26:24,120
was the one who got the best route

456
00:26:24,121 --> 00:26:25,780
and probably won the game.

457
00:26:26,320 --> 00:26:28,920
But winning the game depended on more

458
00:26:28,921 --> 00:26:31,380
than staking out the best route.

459
00:26:31,480 --> 00:26:34,660
The railroads needed settlers more than anything else.

460
00:26:34,760 --> 00:26:36,460
The settlers were their customers.

461
00:26:36,560 --> 00:26:37,560
They were the passengers.

462
00:26:37,660 --> 00:26:39,420
They were the ones who shipped the wheat, who

463
00:26:39,520 --> 00:26:42,260
imported the machinery, who needed the fertilizer.

464
00:26:42,360 --> 00:26:44,900
If it wasn't for the settlers, not thousands,

465
00:26:45,000 --> 00:26:47,180
but millions of them, the railroads

466
00:26:47,280 --> 00:26:48,680
would have had no reason for being.

467
00:26:51,180 --> 00:26:53,620
The government paved the way for settlers

468
00:26:53,720 --> 00:26:57,880
with the Federal Homestead Act of 1862.

469
00:26:57,881 --> 00:27:00,280
American citizens and permanent immigrants

470
00:27:00,380 --> 00:27:04,960
could purchase 160 acres of public land for only $10

471
00:27:05,060 --> 00:27:08,620
after five years of residence.

472
00:27:08,720 --> 00:27:10,920
In order to sell as much land as possible,

473
00:27:11,020 --> 00:27:15,360
railroad companies began competing for the immigrants.

474
00:27:15,460 --> 00:27:18,300
Railroad-sponsored posters, pamphlets, and books

475
00:27:18,400 --> 00:27:21,380
promised religious and political freedom, cheap,

476
00:27:21,480 --> 00:27:25,980
fertile land, and low-priced railroad tickets.

477
00:27:25,981 --> 00:27:29,980
The truth was often stretched or completely ignored.

478
00:27:30,080 --> 00:27:33,480
California, the cornucopia of the world,

479
00:27:33,580 --> 00:27:36,260
room for millions of immigrants and a climate

480
00:27:36,360 --> 00:27:39,600
for health and wealth.

481
00:27:39,700 --> 00:27:42,900
At the same time, the railroad sent hundreds of railroad

482
00:27:43,000 --> 00:27:46,540
pitchmen throughout the East Coast, Europe, and parts of Asia

483
00:27:46,640 --> 00:27:49,840
to recruit as many land-hungry settlers as possible.

484
00:27:49,841 --> 00:27:56,220
In the late 19th century, millions of men, women, and children

485
00:27:56,320 --> 00:28:00,960
from mainly Germany, Ireland, Italy, Scotland, Scandinavia,

486
00:28:01,060 --> 00:28:05,120
and Russia journeyed west by rail, bringing with them

487
00:28:05,220 --> 00:28:08,760
the dream of freedom and opportunity,

488
00:28:08,860 --> 00:28:10,620
and the hope for a better life.

489
00:28:14,640 --> 00:28:18,360
Of the 16 and 1 1 million immigrants who came to America

490
00:28:18,361 --> 00:28:21,260
during the second half of the 19th century.

491
00:28:21,360 --> 00:28:24,500
Almost 11 million immigrants boarded trains

492
00:28:24,600 --> 00:28:27,540
to find their new homes.

493
00:28:27,640 --> 00:28:31,600
Joining the human wave were the legions of American citizens

494
00:28:31,700 --> 00:28:35,580
who wanted to relocate to the West.

495
00:28:35,680 --> 00:28:38,980
The population in the West skyrocketed.

496
00:28:39,080 --> 00:28:42,460
Railroad technology brought some of the fastest, most profound

497
00:28:42,560 --> 00:28:45,480
changes in human history.

498
00:28:45,481 --> 00:28:48,380
The Western railroads did not leave towns.

499
00:28:48,480 --> 00:28:50,220
They created them.

500
00:28:50,320 --> 00:28:53,380
People settled around where the railroad stations were built,

501
00:28:53,480 --> 00:28:57,920
and much of this pattern is still visible today.

502
00:28:58,020 --> 00:29:01,860
The town of Denver, Colorado grew because the railroads chose

503
00:29:01,960 --> 00:29:04,740
it as a junction point at the base of the Rockies.

504
00:29:07,440 --> 00:29:09,440
But hundreds of other Western towns

505
00:29:09,540 --> 00:29:12,140
declined or died if the railroad bosses

506
00:29:12,240 --> 00:29:14,880
built their lines elsewhere.

507
00:29:14,881 --> 00:29:18,120
The settlement of the West brought on by the railroad

508
00:29:18,220 --> 00:29:20,480
radically transformed the land.

509
00:29:20,580 --> 00:29:24,920
Prairies were dug up to create farms for corn and wheat.

510
00:29:25,020 --> 00:29:28,200
Virgin forests were cut for new housing construction

511
00:29:28,300 --> 00:29:31,400
and lumber to build and run the railroad.

512
00:29:31,500 --> 00:29:34,400
All of this growth generated the real moneymaker

513
00:29:34,500 --> 00:29:38,400
for the Western Railroads, freight transportation.

514
00:29:44,880 --> 00:29:48,180
The expansion of the Western Railroad's freight traffic

515
00:29:48,280 --> 00:29:52,420
revolutionized the way America did business.

516
00:29:52,520 --> 00:29:55,520
For the first time, wheat grown on farms in the Midwest

517
00:29:55,620 --> 00:29:59,020
could be shipped to the East Coast at a profit.

518
00:29:59,120 --> 00:30:02,560
Iron ore from Minnesota could be quickly shipped to the blast

519
00:30:02,660 --> 00:30:03,760
furnaces of Pittsburgh.

520
00:30:06,300 --> 00:30:09,300
When the first transcontinental railroad opened,

521
00:30:09,400 --> 00:30:12,400
freight was transported by boxcars and flatcars

522
00:30:12,401 --> 00:30:17,300
and flat cars that hauled everything under the sun.

523
00:30:17,400 --> 00:30:20,980
Within a decade, refrigerated cars were invented.

524
00:30:21,080 --> 00:30:24,020
They were essentially box cars, insulated with cork

525
00:30:24,120 --> 00:30:26,480
and cooled with ice.

526
00:30:26,580 --> 00:30:29,220
Refrigerated cars allowed perishable goods

527
00:30:29,320 --> 00:30:34,560
to be transported across long distances without spoilage.

528
00:30:34,660 --> 00:30:37,600
One of the first left Baltimore, Maryland, for California

529
00:30:37,601 --> 00:30:43,100
with a shipment of fresh Chesapeake Bay oysters.

530
00:30:43,101 --> 00:30:45,100
In that insulated boxcar,

531
00:30:45,101 --> 00:30:49,840
they placed these four large hog-head barrels of oysters.

532
00:30:49,841 --> 00:30:52,440
Around the oysters, they packed ice,

533
00:30:52,441 --> 00:30:55,060
and they had stops along the route

534
00:30:55,061 --> 00:30:56,660
where they could pick up more ice

535
00:30:56,661 --> 00:31:00,160
and pack it tighter with ice as the ice melted.

536
00:31:00,161 --> 00:31:05,360
But that refrigerated cargo got to San Francisco within a week,

537
00:31:05,361 --> 00:31:08,600
And they say that the men were waiting for that,

538
00:31:08,700 --> 00:31:12,160
and they actually were almost auctioning off those oysters.

539
00:31:14,500 --> 00:31:16,540
The advent of the refrigerator car

540
00:31:16,640 --> 00:31:19,980
allowed people to obtain fresh food further and further away

541
00:31:20,080 --> 00:31:22,840
from its source.

542
00:31:22,940 --> 00:31:25,840
Fresh vegetables from California, the far west,

543
00:31:25,940 --> 00:31:28,780
could be transported to every part of the country

544
00:31:28,880 --> 00:31:33,380
at all times of the year, including the dead of winter.

545
00:31:33,381 --> 00:31:37,080
people's eating habits would be changed forever.

546
00:31:37,180 --> 00:31:40,780
The refrigerator car created markets worth fortunes.

547
00:31:40,880 --> 00:31:44,020
It gave rise to the meat packing industry in Chicago,

548
00:31:44,120 --> 00:31:45,820
allowing for dressed and packed meats

549
00:31:45,920 --> 00:31:48,120
to now be transported to local stores

550
00:31:48,220 --> 00:31:51,740
throughout the country.

551
00:31:51,840 --> 00:31:53,840
This vast transportation network

552
00:31:53,940 --> 00:31:56,540
depended on maximum speed and efficiency

553
00:31:56,640 --> 00:32:00,740
and required something we take for granted today, standard time.

554
00:32:03,380 --> 00:32:11,360
In the 19th century, there was no uniform national time

555
00:32:11,460 --> 00:32:12,760
system.

556
00:32:12,860 --> 00:32:15,820
Most people lived their lives according to the sun

557
00:32:15,920 --> 00:32:19,300
and by the ebb and flow of the seasons.

558
00:32:19,400 --> 00:32:22,140
Noon was when the sun passed directly overhead,

559
00:32:22,240 --> 00:32:27,140
which resulted in a complex patchwork of time zones.

560
00:32:27,240 --> 00:32:30,480
When the railroads permitted rapid travel over the continent,

561
00:32:30,481 --> 00:32:35,780
the need for larger unified time zones became critical.

562
00:32:35,880 --> 00:32:40,220
Railroads needed to have precise scheduling where lines crossed

563
00:32:40,320 --> 00:32:45,060
and at stations where trains arrived and departed.

564
00:32:45,160 --> 00:32:47,960
Not having a standardized time system

565
00:32:48,060 --> 00:32:51,160
could actually cause accidents and just simply

566
00:32:51,260 --> 00:32:52,940
make operations inefficient.

567
00:32:55,580 --> 00:32:58,840
In 1883, the major American railroads

568
00:32:58,841 --> 00:33:01,480
held a convention and adopted what they called

569
00:33:01,580 --> 00:33:04,540
railroad standard time, which divided the United States

570
00:33:04,640 --> 00:33:06,180
into four time zones.

571
00:33:08,680 --> 00:33:11,580
On an appointed day, all railroads in America

572
00:33:11,680 --> 00:33:16,360
switched their clocks to follow these new standard time zones.

573
00:33:16,460 --> 00:33:22,560
This completely revolutionized America's concept of time.

574
00:33:22,660 --> 00:33:25,540
In addition to train schedules, the railroads

575
00:33:25,640 --> 00:33:27,800
required extraordinary capital.

576
00:33:27,801 --> 00:33:32,000
more money than any other technology in the 19th century.

577
00:33:32,100 --> 00:33:34,640
The stakes were incredibly high.

578
00:33:34,740 --> 00:33:36,840
The landscape of American business history

579
00:33:36,940 --> 00:33:38,940
is littered with the carcasses of railroads

580
00:33:39,040 --> 00:33:41,640
that seemed like they were a good idea at the time

581
00:33:41,740 --> 00:33:45,060
and failed, or schemes that promoters put together

582
00:33:45,160 --> 00:33:48,960
merely to skin the public and reap the profits and run.

583
00:33:49,060 --> 00:33:50,960
Ultimately, they began falling together

584
00:33:51,060 --> 00:33:54,960
into bigger and bigger systems as these companies consolidated

585
00:33:54,961 --> 00:33:59,960
into actually workable networks in the late 19th century.

586
00:33:59,961 --> 00:34:01,960
One of the biggest networks to emerge

587
00:34:01,961 --> 00:34:04,960
began an ambition that would stop at nothing short

588
00:34:04,961 --> 00:34:08,960
of exploiting America's last untamed wilderness.

589
00:34:17,960 --> 00:34:21,960
One man knew the scent of money and a frontier in America

590
00:34:21,961 --> 00:34:24,960
that had not been exploited, the Great Northern

591
00:34:24,961 --> 00:34:28,560
leading to the Pacific, James Jerome Hill set out

592
00:34:28,660 --> 00:34:31,660
to build what would become the last railroad empire

593
00:34:31,760 --> 00:34:35,400
of the 19th century, the Great Northern Railway.

594
00:34:37,600 --> 00:34:41,600
As a child growing up on a farm in Ontario, Canada,

595
00:34:41,720 --> 00:34:45,520
Jim Hill had lost an eye to a playmate's arrow.

596
00:34:45,620 --> 00:34:50,620
But few men looking westward had as clear a vision.

597
00:34:50,720 --> 00:34:53,660
His battles with rivals won him plenty of nicknames.

598
00:34:53,661 --> 00:35:00,260
Little Giant, Empire Builder, Oregon Bandit, Red River Pirate,

599
00:35:00,360 --> 00:35:02,400
and One-Eyed Old Son of a Bitch.

600
00:35:04,300 --> 00:35:06,300
People who knew him remarked that he

601
00:35:06,400 --> 00:35:09,000
had a mind like a steel trap.

602
00:35:09,100 --> 00:35:10,480
He absorbed information.

603
00:35:10,580 --> 00:35:12,040
He loved facts.

604
00:35:12,140 --> 00:35:14,080
But more than that, he always had a vision.

605
00:35:14,180 --> 00:35:16,720
He set out to be a sailor as a young man,

606
00:35:16,820 --> 00:35:18,680
didn't get farther than the port of New York,

607
00:35:18,780 --> 00:35:21,420
and then headed west, made it to St. Paul, which

608
00:35:21,421 --> 00:35:23,820
which was then a tiny little village at the head

609
00:35:23,920 --> 00:35:26,020
of the Mississippi River navigation,

610
00:35:26,120 --> 00:35:29,560
and remained there the rest of his life.

611
00:35:29,660 --> 00:35:32,960
In 1878, Jim Hill and three other men

612
00:35:33,060 --> 00:35:36,260
acquired the dilapidated St. Paul and Pacific

613
00:35:36,360 --> 00:35:40,180
and set their sights on the Pacific Northwest.

614
00:35:40,280 --> 00:35:42,780
They called their railroad the Great Northern

615
00:35:42,880 --> 00:35:45,880
and pushed west from St. Paul towards Seattle,

616
00:35:45,980 --> 00:35:49,980
building or acquiring track along the way.

617
00:35:49,981 --> 00:35:53,880
JIM HILL WAS THE RINGLEADER AND THE GUIDING FORCE BEHIND IT.

618
00:35:53,980 --> 00:35:56,780
BUT THESE FOUR MEN BUILT IT LARGELY WITH THEIR OWN CAPITAL

619
00:35:56,880 --> 00:35:59,220
AND MONEY THEY WERE ABLE TO RAISE FROM PRIVATE INVESTORS

620
00:35:59,320 --> 00:36:00,420
IN THE EAST.

621
00:36:00,520 --> 00:36:03,520
THEIR PHILOSOPHY WAS TO BUILD WELL, BUILD PERMANENTLY,

622
00:36:03,620 --> 00:36:06,400
AND BUILD A LOT OF TRACK.

623
00:36:06,500 --> 00:36:08,760
UNLIKE OTHER RAILROAD BOSSES OF THE DAY

624
00:36:08,860 --> 00:36:10,960
WHO SAT IN THEIR NEW YORK OFFICES,

625
00:36:11,060 --> 00:36:14,780
HILL PERSONALLY MANAGED THE PROJECT.

626
00:36:14,880 --> 00:36:18,200
UNDER HIS COMMAND, THE GREAT NORTHERN RAILWAY

627
00:36:18,201 --> 00:36:20,940
made incredible progress.

628
00:36:21,040 --> 00:36:25,680
In one season alone, 1887, his army of 10,000 graders

629
00:36:25,780 --> 00:36:29,880
and bridge builders, along with 3,300 teams of horses,

630
00:36:29,980 --> 00:36:34,020
built across 640 miles of the North Dakota and Montana

631
00:36:34,120 --> 00:36:36,660
wilderness.

632
00:36:36,760 --> 00:36:39,280
The biggest obstacle was finding a pass

633
00:36:39,380 --> 00:36:41,260
through the Rocky Mountains.

634
00:36:41,360 --> 00:36:45,200
Hill was convinced there had to be a right pass there.

635
00:36:45,300 --> 00:36:47,660
To tackle the problem, Hill sent his brilliant young

636
00:36:47,661 --> 00:36:51,900
engineer, John F. Stevens.

637
00:36:52,000 --> 00:36:56,060
Stevens reached his goal on December the 11th, 1889,

638
00:36:56,160 --> 00:36:59,900
despite almost freezing to death.

639
00:37:00,000 --> 00:37:04,420
Marius Pass, at an elevation of 5,215 feet,

640
00:37:04,520 --> 00:37:07,820
turned out to be the lowest point on the continental divide

641
00:37:07,920 --> 00:37:12,520
from Canada, south, almost to Mexico.

642
00:37:12,620 --> 00:37:16,620
Along his steel path, Hill created a sophisticated artery

643
00:37:16,621 --> 00:37:21,920
of feeder lines resembling tributaries of a river.

644
00:37:22,020 --> 00:37:25,700
Every few miles, a branch line left the Great Northern's main

645
00:37:25,800 --> 00:37:30,240
track to tap into a potential source of revenue.

646
00:37:30,340 --> 00:37:33,740
In time, he reached the Iron Range of Minnesota,

647
00:37:33,840 --> 00:37:37,240
the copper deposits of Butte, Montana,

648
00:37:37,340 --> 00:37:39,740
the forests of Washington.

649
00:37:39,840 --> 00:37:42,140
Thousands of Great Northern freight trains

650
00:37:42,240 --> 00:37:46,620
would haul these products toward the eastern markets.

651
00:37:46,621 --> 00:37:50,020
A cartoon poked fun of Hill's ambition.

652
00:37:50,120 --> 00:37:52,020
What it didn't capture was his drive

653
00:37:52,120 --> 00:37:55,260
for filling the land with people and jobs,

654
00:37:55,360 --> 00:37:59,360
the key to a railroad success.

655
00:37:59,460 --> 00:38:01,460
He persuaded one friend in Boston

656
00:38:01,560 --> 00:38:04,880
to start an apple orchard in Washington.

657
00:38:04,980 --> 00:38:08,140
That ranch would eventually need four and a half miles

658
00:38:08,240 --> 00:38:13,680
of Great Northern boxcars to ship its annual crop.

659
00:38:13,681 --> 00:38:16,720
Jim Hill encouraged a lumber baron from Minnesota

660
00:38:16,820 --> 00:38:19,660
to launch what would become the vast timber industry

661
00:38:19,760 --> 00:38:22,560
in the Northwest.

662
00:38:22,660 --> 00:38:25,560
Some of the most massive trees on the continent

663
00:38:25,660 --> 00:38:27,160
were chopped down.

664
00:38:27,260 --> 00:38:30,460
Great swatches of pristine forest were cut to clear

665
00:38:30,560 --> 00:38:33,800
right-of-ways for the railroad.

666
00:38:33,900 --> 00:38:38,340
On January 6, 1893, Jim Hill's Great Northern Railway

667
00:38:38,440 --> 00:38:41,180
was officially finished.

668
00:38:41,181 --> 00:38:48,500
After less than 15 years, Hill had built a railroad empire, 25,000 miles of tracks.

669
00:38:49,380 --> 00:38:54,580
Hill soon controlled 33.3% of all the railroad business in the West.

670
00:38:55,600 --> 00:39:04,340
With five transcontinental railroads now completed, the ceremonial striking of the Great Northern's last spike was a mere footnote in history.

671
00:39:05,300 --> 00:39:09,800
It was a bitterly cold and snowy afternoon in the Cascade Mountains.

672
00:39:09,801 --> 00:39:13,240
The workmen only reluctantly left the warmth of their cook

673
00:39:13,340 --> 00:39:17,900
shack to put on a show of celebration for the photographer.

674
00:39:18,000 --> 00:39:21,540
As for the empire builder who did most to bring it about,

675
00:39:21,640 --> 00:39:25,120
he was bedridden with rheumatism in the master bedroom

676
00:39:25,220 --> 00:39:27,020
of his new mansion in St. Paul.

677
00:39:30,020 --> 00:39:32,760
The completion of Hills Railroad symbolized

678
00:39:32,860 --> 00:39:36,760
the end of America's most ambitious building spree.

679
00:39:36,761 --> 00:39:40,360
But the impact of the Western railroads lived on,

680
00:39:40,460 --> 00:39:42,460
ushering in the modern era.

681
00:39:44,460 --> 00:39:47,000
Harvey's rail stops were station restaurants

682
00:39:47,100 --> 00:39:49,800
whose waitresses were a popular attraction.

683
00:39:49,900 --> 00:39:52,000
A Harvey girl had to remain single

684
00:39:52,100 --> 00:39:54,120
for the first year of employment.

685
00:39:54,220 --> 00:39:56,920
If she married, her salary was cut in half.

686
00:40:00,720 --> 00:40:04,860
The Chicago World's Fair in 1893 represented the zenith

687
00:40:04,861 --> 00:40:07,500
of 19th century American technology,

688
00:40:07,600 --> 00:40:10,260
marking the true end of the last century

689
00:40:10,360 --> 00:40:14,700
and the dawn of our modern era.

690
00:40:14,800 --> 00:40:17,840
Among those present were three of the greatest visionaries

691
00:40:17,940 --> 00:40:19,380
of the time.

692
00:40:19,480 --> 00:40:24,080
George Westinghouse, who invented the air brakes, was there.

693
00:40:24,180 --> 00:40:26,340
His electrical system lighted the fare,

694
00:40:26,440 --> 00:40:31,020
astounding millions of faregoers with its brilliant glow.

695
00:40:31,120 --> 00:40:34,260
He spent his last years devising the alternating current

696
00:40:34,261 --> 00:40:38,500
technology that revolutionized America.

697
00:40:38,600 --> 00:40:42,640
George Pullman was there with his finest cars that epitomized

698
00:40:42,740 --> 00:40:45,940
the level of comfort, safety, and prestige

699
00:40:46,040 --> 00:40:49,580
that America had attained as a society.

700
00:40:49,680 --> 00:40:53,340
And Jim Hill was there with his great northern locomotives

701
00:40:53,440 --> 00:40:55,780
representing the Western expansionism

702
00:40:55,880 --> 00:40:58,520
that the fair was celebrating.

703
00:40:58,620 --> 00:41:02,420
Five transcontinental railroads and hundreds of feeder lines

704
00:41:02,421 --> 00:41:05,420
the 200,000 miles of steel had been a colossal feat,

705
00:41:05,421 --> 00:41:08,420
a testament to the power of technology

706
00:41:08,421 --> 00:41:11,420
and man's ambition to conquer the West.

707
00:41:11,421 --> 00:41:14,420
As the 19th century drew to a close,

708
00:41:14,421 --> 00:41:18,420
the pioneering iron horse was now America's first big business.

709
00:41:18,421 --> 00:41:23,420
A vast industrial complex manufactured steam locomotives,

710
00:41:23,421 --> 00:41:25,420
freight and passenger cars,

711
00:41:25,421 --> 00:41:29,420
and every other need to build and run a railroad.

712
00:41:29,421 --> 00:41:33,600
and every other need to build and run a railroad.

713
00:41:33,700 --> 00:41:36,860
One major industry that grew up as a result of the railroad

714
00:41:36,960 --> 00:41:39,960
was America's steel industry.

715
00:41:40,060 --> 00:41:43,140
Before steel, railroads used expensive wrought iron

716
00:41:43,240 --> 00:41:44,240
for track building.

717
00:41:44,340 --> 00:41:47,380
But the railroad's demand for a strong, durable ally

718
00:41:47,480 --> 00:41:49,880
encouraged the growth of the steel industry

719
00:41:49,980 --> 00:41:51,940
in the 1870s and 1880s.

720
00:41:55,320 --> 00:41:57,980
One lasting legacy of the Western railroads

721
00:41:57,981 --> 00:41:59,980
is hard to believe.

722
00:42:00,080 --> 00:42:02,880
Ironically, after three decades of promoting

723
00:42:02,980 --> 00:42:05,980
Western expansionism, the railroads lobbied

724
00:42:06,080 --> 00:42:08,600
for conservation of many natural treasures

725
00:42:08,700 --> 00:42:12,800
that they had made accessible to the public for the first time.

726
00:42:12,900 --> 00:42:15,900
They realized that they had opened a Pandora's box

727
00:42:16,000 --> 00:42:20,440
and that they were permitting the kind of despoilation,

728
00:42:20,540 --> 00:42:23,500
the kind of loss of this wonderful scenery,

729
00:42:23,600 --> 00:42:25,840
and that if they themselves didn't protect it,

730
00:42:25,940 --> 00:42:27,200
no one else would.

731
00:42:27,201 --> 00:42:30,400
And if they lost it, the nation lost it, the world lost it,

732
00:42:30,500 --> 00:42:33,200
it was gone forever.

733
00:42:33,300 --> 00:42:34,940
In the years to come, the railroads

734
00:42:35,040 --> 00:42:39,440
played a major role in creating the national park system.

735
00:42:39,540 --> 00:42:43,180
The Grand Canyon, Yosemite, Yellowstone,

736
00:42:43,280 --> 00:42:46,220
and other great natural wonders.

737
00:42:46,320 --> 00:42:49,760
Their grandeur lives on today, long after the time

738
00:42:49,860 --> 00:42:53,420
the steam engine was replaced by diesel.

739
00:42:53,520 --> 00:42:56,000
Many of the tracks which ran across America

740
00:42:56,001 --> 00:42:58,640
are now collecting weeds and rust.

741
00:42:58,740 --> 00:43:01,580
Railroads still haul millions of tons of freight

742
00:43:01,680 --> 00:43:02,780
across the country.

743
00:43:02,880 --> 00:43:06,080
But for the few people who now make the journey by rail,

744
00:43:06,180 --> 00:43:11,040
the elegance and mystique have long vanished.

745
00:43:11,140 --> 00:43:15,380
Today, Pullman cars are a memory, eclipsed in the 1930s

746
00:43:15,480 --> 00:43:17,360
by the airplane.

747
00:43:17,460 --> 00:43:20,520
But they would enjoy one last moment of glory,

748
00:43:20,620 --> 00:43:23,100
carrying Harry Truman across America

749
00:43:23,101 --> 00:43:26,000
in his 1948 whistle stop campaign.

750
00:43:31,740 --> 00:43:34,880
The legacy of the thousands of men who worked in the West

751
00:43:34,980 --> 00:43:37,120
on the railroad lives on.

752
00:43:37,220 --> 00:43:41,480
Their names may be lost to time, but their sweat and sacrifice

753
00:43:41,580 --> 00:43:45,180
helped build a nation out of the wilderness,

754
00:43:45,280 --> 00:43:49,260
land built on freedom, opportunity, dreams,

755
00:43:49,261 --> 00:43:53,560
and thousands of miles of steel rail.

